description:
As a site visitor
I want to browse and read blog posts on the marketing site
So that I can learn about product updates and content that helps me decide to engage further

Context

Adds a Blogs section to the marketing site, backed by a new Payload CMS content collection. Posts are pre-rendered as static pages at build time for performance and SEO.

Acceptance Criteria

- A Payload CMS collection exists for blog posts with fields for title, slug, excerpt, body, publish date, and author
- All published blog posts are pre-rendered as static pages at build time (no client-side fetch required to view content)
- A Blog Listing page shows all published posts (title, excerpt, publish date) and supports keyword search
- Clicking a post on the listing page opens a public blog detail page rendering the full Payload CMS content
- Draft/unpublished posts in Payload CMS do not appear on the listing or detail pages
- Blog listing and detail pages render correctly on mobile and desktop breakpoints


Out of Scope

- Comments, likes, or social sharing on blog posts


- Custom authoring UI beyond Payload's built-in admin panel


- Tagging/category taxonomy and related-posts recommendations


- RSS feed generation




Dependencies

- None — assumes the existing Payload CMS + Neon setup in apps/marketing is already available




Technical Reference — Schema

Schema below is the agreed implementation target, adapted from Payload's official website template. Deviations from that template are marked inline and explained under Schema notes.

Reference: payloadcms/payload — templates/website

// apps/marketing — src/collections/Posts.ts
// Derived from the official Payload website template, with `categories` and
// `relatedPosts` removed per this ticket's Out of Scope.
import type { CollectionConfig } from 'payload'
import {
  BlocksFeature, FixedToolbarFeature, HeadingFeature,
  HorizontalRuleFeature, InlineToolbarFeature, lexicalEditor,
} from '@payloadcms/richtext-lexical'
import {
  MetaDescriptionField, MetaImageField, MetaTitleField,
  OverviewField, PreviewField,
} from '@payloadcms/plugin-seo/fields'

import { authenticated } from '../access/authenticated'
import { authenticatedOrPublished } from '../access/authenticatedOrPublished'
import { Banner } from '../blocks/Banner/config'
import { Code } from '../blocks/Code/config'
import { MediaBlock } from '../blocks/MediaBlock/config'
import { populateAuthors } from './Posts/hooks/populateAuthors'
import { revalidateDelete, revalidatePost } from './Posts/hooks/revalidatePost'

export const Posts: CollectionConfig = {
  slug: 'posts',
  access: {
    create: authenticated,
    update: authenticated,
    delete: authenticated,
    // AC5 in one line: unauthenticated readers see published docs only.
    read: authenticatedOrPublished,
  },
  defaultPopulate: {
    title: true,
    slug: true,
    excerpt: true,
    meta: { image: true, description: true },
  },
  admin: {
    useAsTitle: 'title',
    defaultColumns: ['title', 'slug', 'publishedAt', 'updatedAt'],
  },
  fields: [
    { name: 'title', type: 'text', required: true },
    {
      // AC1/AC3. The upstream template has NO excerpt field -- it reuses
      // meta.description. A distinct field is used here because AC3 asks the
      // listing to show an excerpt, and coupling that to the SEO description
      // makes one field serve two audiences. See "Schema notes" below: this
      // field MUST also be added to the search index.
      name: 'excerpt',
      type: 'textarea',
      required: true,
      maxLength: 300,
      admin: { description: 'Shown on the blog listing card. Plain text.' },
    },
    {
      type: 'tabs',
      tabs: [
        {
          label: 'Content',
          fields: [
            { name: 'heroImage', type: 'upload', relationTo: 'media' },
            {
              name: 'content',
              type: 'richText',
              required: true,
              label: false,
              editor: lexicalEditor({
                features: ({ rootFeatures }) => [
                  ...rootFeatures,
                  HeadingFeature({ enabledHeadingSizes: ['h1', 'h2', 'h3', 'h4'] }),
                  BlocksFeature({ blocks: [Banner, Code, MediaBlock] }),
                  FixedToolbarFeature(),
                  InlineToolbarFeature(),
                  HorizontalRuleFeature(),
                ],
              }),
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          name: 'meta',
          label: 'SEO',
          fields: [
            OverviewField({
              titlePath: 'meta.title',
              descriptionPath: 'meta.description',
              imagePath: 'meta.image',
            }),
            MetaTitleField({ hasGenerateFn: true }),
            MetaImageField({ relationTo: 'media' }),
            MetaDescriptionField({}),
            PreviewField({
              hasGenerateFn: true,
              titlePath: 'meta.title',
              descriptionPath: 'meta.description',
            }),
          ],
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      name: 'publishedAt',
      type: 'date',
      admin: {
        position: 'sidebar',
        date: { pickerAppearance: 'dayAndTime' },
      },
      hooks: {
        beforeChange: [
          ({ siblingData, value }) => {
            if (siblingData._status === 'published' && !value) return new Date()
            return value
          },
        ],
      },
    },
    {
      name: 'authors',
      type: 'relationship',
      relationTo: 'users',
      hasMany: true,
      admin: { position: 'sidebar' },
    },
    {
      // Required, not optional. The users collection locks read access for
      // privacy, so the `authors` relationship returns no names to the public
      // API. This hidden array is filled by the populateAuthors afterRead hook
      // and is what the frontend renders. Omit it and author names render
      // empty in production while passing an authenticated smoke test.
      name: 'populatedAuthors',
      type: 'array',
      access: { update: () => false },
      admin: { disabled: true, readOnly: true },
      fields: [
        { name: 'id', type: 'text' },
        { name: 'name', type: 'text' },
      ],
    },
    { name: 'slug', type: 'slug', useAsSlug: 'title' },
  ],
  hooks: {
    afterChange: [revalidatePost],
    afterRead: [populateAuthors],
    afterDelete: [revalidateDelete],
  },
  versions: {
    drafts: { autosave: { interval: 100 }, schedulePublish: true },
    maxPerDoc: 50,
  },
}

Search configuration

// payload.config.ts -- AC3 keyword search.
// searchOverrides/beforeSync must carry `excerpt`, or the listing shows
// excerpt text that keyword search cannot match.
searchPlugin({
  collections: ['posts'],
  beforeSync: beforeSyncWithSearch,
  searchOverrides: {
    fields: ({ defaultFields }) => [...defaultFields, ...searchFields],
  },
})

// src/search/beforeSync.ts
export const beforeSyncWithSearch: BeforeSync = async ({ originalDoc, searchDoc }) => {
  const { slug, id, title, excerpt, meta } = originalDoc
  return {
    ...searchDoc,
    slug,
    excerpt,
    meta: {
      ...meta,
      title: meta?.title || title,
      image: meta?.image?.id || meta?.image,
      description: meta?.description,
    },
  }
}

Schema notes

- excerpt is an addition, not a copy. The upstream template has no excerpt field and reuses meta.description. AC3 needs a listing excerpt, so a dedicated required textarea (max 300) is used instead of overloading the SEO description.


- populatedAuthors is load-bearing, not boilerplate. The users collection locks read access, so the authors relationship returns no names to unauthenticated callers. Without the hidden array and its populateAuthors afterRead hook, author names render empty in production while passing an authenticated test.


- AC2 is satisfied by generateStaticParams plus the revalidatePost afterChange hook: pages are pre-rendered at build and re-rendered on-demand when a post changes. No client-side fetch is used to render post content either way.


- AC5 needs no bespoke filtering. access.read: authenticatedOrPublished combined with versions.drafts excludes drafts from both the listing and the detail route.


- Deliberately omitted from the template, per Out of Scope: the categories relationship and Categories collection, relatedPosts and the RelatedPosts block, and nestedDocsPlugin. Dropping categories also removes the category-population loop from beforeSync.


- Media and Users collections and the access/ helpers are reused from the template as-is; only Posts is new.
